# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# Qt and the glib event dispatcher it uses ship uninstrumented, so the
# happens-before edges they establish — the QDBusConnection thread's handoff in
# particular — are invisible to TSan, and every object one of them allocates on
# one thread and frees on another reads as a race between its own new and its
# own delete.
#
# `called_from_lib` suppresses TSan's INTERCEPTORS when the named library is the
# immediate caller. A race in Dish's own code, or in an interceptor Dish calls,
# still reports. Do not widen this to `race:` patterns.
called_from_lib:libQt6Core.so
called_from_lib:libQt6DBus.so
# The HTTP thread QNetworkAccessManager runs the manifest fetch on.
called_from_lib:libQt6Network.so
# The TLS stack is uninstrumented too, and it works on both sides of a thread
# boundary of QNetworkAccessManager's own making: its HTTP thread runs the
# handshake and parses the peer certificate inside OpenSSL, then hands the
# certificate to the owner thread through a queued signal, whose ordering is
# a QMutex and an eventfd inside Qt that TSan cannot see. Every read of that
# certificate on the owner thread, the TOFU pin check first of all, then
# reports as a race inside libcrypto: i2d reading the encoding that d2i
# cached on the other thread. First seen when test_pairing_wire became the
# first test to drive TLS under this lane; every HTTPClient call has always
# crossed this boundary.
# The three are listed together on purpose. libcrypto alone (2026-09-23) hid
# this and surfaced the plugin's own new on one thread and delete on the other
# as races in Moonlight session tests that had passed before.
# Caveat: a race whose ONLY witness is inside the TLS stack, two threads
# driving one QSslSocket say, is hidden too. First-party accesses still report,
# and libsodium, SDL and every other library stay visible.
# Interim: the fix that needs none of these is a transport that does not cross
# a thread (D:\TinkerNorth\DISH_TLS_TRANSPORT_PROMPT.md, outside the repo).
called_from_lib:libqopensslbackend.so
called_from_lib:libssl.so
called_from_lib:libcrypto.so
# Qt 6.9 reimplemented QWaitCondition over std::condition_variable, so a Qt
# thread handoff now enters the interceptor through libstdc++'s out-of-line
# condition_variable::wait instead of a libQt6* frame, and the lines above no
# longer match: TSan then reports "unlock of an unlocked mutex" against a
# mutex living inside Qt-allocated memory it never saw locked (seen from
# QNetworkAccessManager's worker startup under test_moonlight_binding_refcount).
# Safe ONLY while no first-party code uses std::condition_variable — both of
# those waits would be silenced too. Grep src/ before keeping this line.
called_from_lib:libstdc++.so
called_from_lib:libglib-2.0.so
called_from_lib:libgobject-2.0.so
called_from_lib:libdbus-1.so
